        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Navajo Tens',
            subtitle: 'Fishing',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Navajo Tens is a double-deck Conquian variant, called Neezn?? Dah Y?jih? in Navajo. Players meld runs and sets, must immediately meld any card they take, and win by melding an eleventh card.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Traditional Navajo card game played among Navajo communities in the USA. The Navajo name is Neeznáá Dah Yíjihí or sometimes Da'aka' (general word for card game). A documented variant uses a double deck with 80 cards and meld-11 Conquian-style play for 2–7 players; fishing-style capture versions summing to ten are also played in Navajo communities.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two or more players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard deck',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Four cards dealt to each player, four cards face-up in the center. Remaining cards form the draw pile.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
